+{
+ "note": "Class-summary questions with no single drug home. Candidate placements: class page quiz section, spaced-repetition mixed deck, or Custom Test 'mixed set'.",
+ "questions": [
+  {
+   "source": "KYP_ADJUNCT_MCQ_Batch_16b.json",
+   "id": "adj-mix-01",
+   "question": "Match each drug-induced movement or autonomic problem to its classic remedy (the KYP EPS-treatment map): 1. Acute dystonia. 2. Antipsychotic-induced akathisia. 3. Drug-induced parkinsonism. 4. Tardive dyskinesia.",
+   "options": [
+    "1-IM/IV benztropine or promethazine; 2-propranolol; 3-oral trihexyphenidyl or amantadine; 4-valbenazine or deutetrabenazine, with anticholinergics AVOIDED",
+    "1-propranolol; 2-IM benztropine; 3-valbenazine; 4-oral trihexyphenidyl",
+    "1-oral trihexyphenidyl; 2-valbenazine; 3-propranolol; 4-IM benztropine",
+    "1-valbenazine; 2-amantadine; 3-IM benztropine; 4-propranolol"
+   ],
+   "correctIndex": 0,
+   "explanation": "The map is fixed: acute dystonia gets a parenteral anticholinergic (benztropine or promethazine) within minutes; akathisia is the propranolol (or benzodiazepine) problem; established drug-induced parkinsonism takes an oral anticholinergic or occasional amantadine; and tardive dyskinesia — the trap where anticholinergics worsen movements — needs VMAT2 inhibition. Each distractor deliberately rotates these pairings; only the correct pairing reproduces the map exactly.",
+   "afterSectionId": "knowledge-graph"
+  },
+  {
+   "source": "KYP_ATYPICAL2_MCQ_Batch_07.json",
+   "id": "atd-mix-b07-01",
+   "question": "Match each atypical antidepressant to its signature hallmark — which option contains only correct pairings?",
+   "options": [
+    "Vilazodone — mandatory LFT monitoring; Vortioxetine — must be taken with food; Agomelatine — studied for cognition in MDD",
+    "Tianeptine — pure NRI never FDA-approved; Reboxetine — mu-opioid agonism with abuse potential; Atomoxetine — melatonin agonism for shift-work depression",
+    "Vilazodone — must be taken with food; Vortioxetine — studied for cognition in MDD, dose halved with strong CYP2D6 inhibitors; Agomelatine — MT1/MT2 agonism with mandatory LFT monitoring",
+    "Tianeptine — first-line ADHD therapy in children; Reboxetine — potent 5-HT3 antagonism; Atomoxetine — serotonin reuptake enhancer"
+   ],
+   "correctIndex": 2,
+   "explanation": "The six signatures in one line: vilazodone = food-dependent absorption (the SPARI), vortioxetine = cognition with 2D6-driven dose halving, agomelatine = melatonin receptors plus LFT surveillance, tianeptine = mu-opioid agonism and abuse, reboxetine = pure NRI never FDA-approved, atomoxetine = non-stimulant ADHD therapy. Each wrong option swaps a hallmark onto a neighbouring drug — the standard tag-swap trap.",
+   "afterSectionId": "knowledge-graph"
+  },
+  {
+   "source": "KYP_ATYPICALAP2_MCQ_Batch_11.json",
+   "id": "atp-mix-b11-01",
+   "question": "Signature-feature matching across this atypical-antipsychotic class — which pairing is correct?",
+   "options": [
+    "Asenapine — sublingual only, since swallowed tablets are destroyed by first-pass metabolism",
+    "Ziprasidone — the highest weight-gain agent of the class",
+    "Cariprazine — the shortest effective half-life in the class",
+    "Amisulpride — FDA-approved in the USA for bipolar depression"
+   ],
+   "correctIndex": 0,
+   "explanation": "Asenapine is sublingual only because swallowed tablets are destroyed by first-pass metabolism — the swallow-kills-bioavailability pearl. Ziprasidone is instead among the LEANEST metabolic agents, cariprazine has the class's longest effective half-life (~1–3 weeks with its didesmethyl metabolite), and amisulpride is not FDA-approved (EU/India availability, Katzung). Every distractor inverts a real signature.",
+   "afterSectionId": "high-yield-summary"
+  },
+  {
+   "source": "KYP_ATYPICALAP_MCQ_Batch_10.json",
+   "id": "atp-mix-01",
+   "question": "Which drug—signature-liability pair from the 'big five' atypical antipsychotics is correctly matched?",
+   "options": [
+    "Risperidone — the most prolactin-elevating atypical of the group",
+    "Quetiapine — highest EPS liability among the five",
+    "Aripiprazole — the highest weight-gain and diabetes risk",
+    "Olanzapine — the lowest metabolic liability in the class"
+   ],
+   "correctIndex": 0,
+   "explanation": "Risperidone (with paliperidone) stands apart for prolactin elevation — galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, gynaecomastia and long-term osteoporosis risk — the defining class-contrast pearl. Quetiapine is actually the EPS-sparing one (alongside clozapine), aripiprazole is the leanest of the five, and olanzapine shares the metabolic top with clozapine — each of the other options deliberately swaps the superlative onto the wrong drug.",
+   "afterSectionId": "high-yield-summary"
+  },
+  {
+   "source": "KYP_ATYPICAL_MCQ_Batch_06.json",
+   "id": "atd-mix-01",
+   "question": "Match each atypical antidepressant to its signature safety red flag — which mapping is correct?",
+   "options": [
+    "Bupropion — agranulocytosis; mirtazapine — seizures; trazodone — weight gain; nefazodone — priapism",
+    "Bupropion — weight gain; mirtazapine — insomnia; mianserin — sexual dysfunction; trazodone — hypertension",
+    "Bupropion — dose-related seizures; mirtazapine — sedation and weight gain; mianserin — blood dyscrasias; trazodone — priapism; nefazodone — hepatotoxicity",
+    "Bupropion — priapism; mirtazapine — hepatotoxicity; mianserin — seizures; trazodone — agranulocytosis"
+   ],
+   "correctIndex": 2,
+   "explanation": "The one-line safety map every exam wants: bupropion = dose-related seizures (avoid eating disorders and seizure history), mirtazapine = sedation with appetite/weight gain, mianserin = blood dyscrasias (fever/sore throat demands a CBC), trazodone = priapism and orthostatic hypotension, nefazodone = black-box hepatotoxicity. Every other row has shifted a red flag onto the wrong drug.",
+   "afterSectionId": "knowledge-graph"
